From gander@voyager.netcomi.com Wed Feb  5 14:19:24 1997
Message-ID: <201702051800.MAA01075@voyager.netcomi.com>
Date: Sun, 05 Feb 2017 12:00:43 +0600
From: RHS Linux User <gander@voyager.netcomi.com>
Subject: [FAQ HOW-TO] Idiot's Guide to Running Netscape on AXP/Linux (V 2.0)

Idiot's Guide to Running Netscape on Linux/AXP 

by Gerald Anderson (gander@voyager.netcomi.com)

Version 2.0							3/5/97

Introduction:

	Support for running ECOFF (the OSF and DU version of a.out) binaries
have been built into the Linux/AXP kernels.  In effect, this allows you to run
with minimal difficulty a large variety of programs that were orginally 
compiled on an OSF/1 or Digital Unix alpha (AXP).

Requirements:

	There are two seperate lists of requirements depending on if you have
access to OSF/1 3.[02] or Digital Unix 4.0.

	For OSF
	-------
	Linux kernel version 2.[01].x (You must have ECOFF support compiled in)
	Access to an OSF/1 3.[02] alpha
	
	For DU
	______
	Linux kernel version 2.1.23+ (You must have ECOFF support compiled in)
	Access to a DU 4.0 alpha

IMPORTANT NOTE:  All Digital software mentioned in this document is copyright
  Digital.  You cannot legally use any of this software on your Linux box
  without first having the appropriate Digital License.

How-To:

	For both OSF/1 3.[02] and Digital Unix 4.0 you must complete the 
following.

1.  Get /sbin/loader from the Digital machine and place it in /sbin/loader
	on your Linux box.

2.  Create a directory on your Linux box for /usr/shlib

3.  Get the following libraries from the Digital box and place in the 
	/usr/shlib directory on your Linux box

		libXm.so
		libXmu.so
		libm.so
		libX11.so
		libXt.so
		libdnet_stub.so
		libc.so
		libXext.so

[If you are going to use Digital Unix 4.0 libs/loader you must also get]

		libSM.so
		libICE.so

4.  Get the file /etc/svc.conf from the Digital box and place it in 
	/etc/svc.conf on your Linux box.

5.  Download from ftp.netscape.com the OSF/Alpha version of navigator
	(ie. netscape-v301-export.alpha-dec-osf2.0.tar.gz)

6.  Install it

7.  Run it!!	

Conclusion:

  This procedure has been tested on a RedHat 4.0 and RedHat 4.1 box with 
kernel versions 2.0.27, 2.0.28 (for OSF/1 3.[02]) and 2.1.23 (for DU 4.0). 
It seems to be very stable when running the OSF/1 libs/loader but I've noticed
some problems with Segmentation Faults using the DU 4.0 libs/loader.  I've
also noticed that when using DU 4.0 libs/loader that the /etc/svc.conf file
is not 'as' necessary as when running the OSF/1 stuff, It does complain, but
it is not fatal.

  If you have questions regarding this document please first refer to the
archives for the axp-list mailing list at http://www.redhat.com, then if you
didn't find your answer feel free to mail me at gander@voyager.netcomi.com.

  All other comments regarding this document may be sent directly to 
gander@voyager.netcomi.com

NOTE:  Java applets do not work with any version of OSF/1 or DU libs/loader.
I have not heard if anybody is working on this, or what the problem is.

Following is a note recieved from Linus regarding support for DU 4.0 
libs/loader, if you do email him, please insure as noted that the problem
is detailed and reproducible. 

Notes about using Digital Unix 4.0

[From axp-list@redhat.com]

Please report any problems wrt Digital UNIX binary compatibility to me,
and I'll look into them (assuming the report is detailed and reproducible
enough). There is a known problem with initial argument handling: argv[0]
is /sbin/loader instead of the program we actually run, and I haven't
looked into this one yet (and currently that's pretty low on my list of
things to look at..). This problem _may_ be related to netscape getting a
SIGSEGV if you start it without any arguments (but if you give it
arguments it works fine at least for me). 

		Linus

Enjoy,

Gerald Anderson

