From d-champion@uchicago.edu Tue Feb 18 17:39:08 1997
Message-ID: <199702182232.QAA10084@melanoma.uchicago.edu>
Date: Tue, 18 Feb 1997 16:32:06 -0600 (CST)
From: D. Champion <d-champion@uchicago.edu>
Subject: Re: fclose()
In-Reply-To: <19970218191335.5758.qmail@brouhaha.com>

>> I expect fclose to give me an error return - something more gracefull than a
>> segfault. Fclose should know that the ptr is invalid - points to no open
>> channel - and give an appropiate error code.
>
>What's the point in checking the return code or errno for fclose()?  What
>are you going to do if it fails, try to close it again, hoping that the error
>condition has been remedied?  Print "Abort, Retry, Ignore?" to stderr?  :-)

Oh, so -you're- the guy who does that.

One reason off the top of my head is that the f* stdio suite is
buffered, and a failed fclose() can indicate failure to flush pending
output.  Maybe you differ, but I want to know when my newly-compiled
kernel image didn't complete its copy to /vmunix.gz because / is 40
bytes too small.

-D.

